# Patch numbers are permanent IDs: this file alone sets the order, and merged
# patches keep the number of the one they absorbed the others into. A patch's
# name starts with the part of Samba it changes; the headings say why.
#
# Whole source files that do not exist upstream live in overlay/, mirroring the
# Samba tree, and are copied in before the first patch (patch_copy_overlay).
# Patches change upstream files only; never add or edit an overlay file here.
#
# The patch files are generated: edit them through build/samba4x_replay.py
# (init, amend, export, verify), never by hand.

# Build and toolchain.
# Samba 4.25 marks some generated tools as hostcc but still lets waf inherit
# target compiler/linker settings. Keep those host tools on the VM compiler.
0001-waf-hostcc-target-flags.patch|waf hostcc isolation from target flags
# Apply static link flags only to the static binaries we build: the deployed
# smbd (with its link map), the other shipped binary, the metadata migrator,
# by name, and the opt-in tc_*_test regression drivers, which never ship.
0049-waf-static-appliance-binaries.patch|static link flags for smbd, the migrator and regression drivers
# Upstream repeats several typedefs across headers (SMB_STRUCT_STAT and
# files_struct, Heimdal's heim_pcontext and krb5int32). C11 and GCC 4.6 and
# later accept a repeated typedef; the NetBSD 4 SDK's GCC 4.1.2 rejects it, so
# guard each one.
0014-includes-typedef-guards.patch|duplicate typedef guards
# NetBSD 7's <glob.h> (the NetBSD 6 lane's SDK) declares glob_pattern_p(),
# which conflicts with bundled popt's static fallback of the same name ("static
# follows non-static"); rename popt's copy. The NetBSD 4 headers lack the
# declaration.
0016-popt-glob-symbol-rename.patch|bundled popt glob symbol rename
# Rc2 uses C11 generic selection for const-preserving charset helpers. The
# existing NetBSD SDK GCC predates that syntax; retain the types with GNU builtins.
0036-charset-legacy-gcc-generic.patch|const-preserving charset helpers on legacy GCC

# NetBSD 4 and old-kernel compatibility.
# NetBSD 4 libc lacks the *at family, futimens, utimensat, arc4random_buf and
# getline; replace.c includes shims for them (overlay
# lib/replace/tc_netbsd4_compat.c), and system/filesys.h gains O_DIRECTORY and
# AT_* fallback defines.
0002-replace-netbsd4-compat.patch|NetBSD4 libc compatibility shims
# Apple's NetBSD 6.0 kernel returns ENOSYS for openat, fstatat, mkdirat,
# unlinkat and readlinkat although the NetBSD 7 SDK declares them (probed on the
# device 2026-09-27), and NetBSD 4 has none of them. So on every lane
# vfs_default's *at calls fchdir() to the directory's descriptor and use the
# path-based syscalls (overlay source3/modules/tc_vfs_at_compat.c). The
# fallbacks report NetBSD's EFTYPE for O_NOFOLLOW on a symlink as ELOOP, which
# Samba expects. NetBSD 4 also has no fdopendir(), so there only, directories
# are opened by name, and vfs_catia gains an fdopendir hook so that name is the
# on-disk (SFM-mapped) one.
0003-vfs-default-path-fallbacks.patch|Time Capsule VFS runtime syscall fallbacks
# Samba's default streaminfo refreshes stat before appending ::$DATA. The
# pathref fallback can fail that refresh even when the open path already
# cached valid stat metadata; use the cached stat for macOS delete/share checks.
0050-vfs-default-streaminfo-cached-stat.patch|keep the default stream when the pathref stat refresh fails
# NetBSD4 can lose Samba's cached is_directory flag even when stat still says
# the handle is a directory; recover before rejecting SMB2 QUERY_DIRECTORY.
0004-smb2-query-directory-stat-fallback.patch|NetBSD4 SMB2 directory stat fallback
# NetBSD 4 has no posix_spawn (no spawn.h), so its lanes (TC_SAMBA4X_NETBSD4_COMPAT)
# fork and execve instead. Samba's configure never defines HAVE_POSIX_SPAWN, so
# gate on the lane, not on that. Apple's NetBSD 6.0 kernel has posix_spawn(2)
# (tested on the device 2026-09-27), and the NetBSD 6 lane uses it. local_np.c
# is live: every pipe but srvsvc (served inside smbd, 0005) tries to start
# samba-dcerpcd, which the appliance does not ship; posix_spawn fails with
# ENOENT and no child, the fallback after a fork. queue_process.c only has to
# compile, as printing is disabled.
0015-spawn-fork-exec-fallback.patch|posix_spawn fallback on NetBSD 4
# NetBSD 4 lacks O_CLOEXEC, which tdb_wrap passes to tdb_open; define it as 0
# in tdb_wrap.c only (tdb_open_ex() sets FD_CLOEXEC itself). A global define
# would make other code skip its fcntl(FD_CLOEXEC) fallback.
0007-tdb-wrap-o-cloexec.patch|O_CLOEXEC for tdb_wrap on NetBSD 4
# NetBSD distinguishes ENOATTR from ENODATA. Missing optional extents must not
# fail stream cleanup after its primary xattr is gone; retain real unlink errors.
0035-streams-xattr-enoattr.patch|portable stream extent cleanup and unlink errors
# On every NetBSD lane, read the interfaces from sysctl(NET_RT_IFLIST), decoded
# by each message's RTM_VERSION, as the native service does (overlay
# lib/socket/tc_netbsd_iflist.c). libc getifaddrs() misparses Apple's NetBSD 4
# if_msghdr (152 bytes against the SDK's 144), and Samba's SIOCGIFCONF
# replacement assumes fixed-size ifreq rows, which that kernel does not return.
# Nothing in smbd calls getifaddrs() afterwards. _samba4x.sh still clears
# configure's getifaddrs results and forces the IFCONF backend after configure
# (which runs its interface probes either way); that is left over from before
# this patch and harmless.
0043-interfaces-netbsd-route-iflist.patch|parse Time Capsule interfaces from routing messages

# No-pthread build.
# Without HAVE_PTHREAD, pthreadpool_tevent still used pthread mutexes for its
# per-event-context glue, and tevent_threads.c's context destructor still
# locked a mutex.
# Guard those on HAVE_PTHREAD. The poll_mt backend needs no pthread and stays
# registered; nothing here selects it. Pthread builds are unchanged.
0051-tevent-no-pthread-build.patch|build tevent and pthreadpool_tevent without pthread
# Disable pthread before libreplace constructs the dependency graph and define
# __thread away. --disable-pthread alone already selects the synchronous
# pthreadpool backend; _samba4x.sh also passes --disable-pthreadpool so
# source3/wscript does not add vfs_aio_pthread. Without threads,
# thread-local storage is process storage, and in the static appliance binary
# any real __thread access from Samba's -fPIC objects calls static libc's
# __tls_get_addr, which only aborts. That abort was the startup crash in
# tevent_req_create (tevent's call-depth state) and in the winbind SID lookup
# (libwbclient's client name) that earlier patches avoided; the NetBSD 4
# compilers never enabled __thread, only the NetBSD 6 lane did.
0013-configure-disable-pthread.patch|configure --disable-pthread; __thread defined away
# Without pthread, talloc_stack frees its frame tracker from an atexit handler
# that logs every frame still open, so every smbd connection child logged about
# three "Dangling frame" lines and smbd -V/-b did too. pthread builds never run
# that report at exit(); free the tracker the same way, quietly, and clear the
# pointer so a later exit handler starts a new tracker. This also fixes an
# upstream NULL dereference: talloc_stackframe_exists() (smb2_signing.c)
# registers the handler before any frame exists, and upstream's handler then
# reads the NULL tracker at exit.
0022-talloc-stack-quiet-exit.patch|quiet talloc stack teardown at exit

# Appliance kernel and disk.
# Apple's NetBSD 4 and NetBSD 6 kernels lose writes to a static binary's
# initialized data: handling the first write to a .data page, UVM fault-ahead
# maps the neighbouring pages (4 below, 3 above) from the executable again,
# dropping this process's (or its parent's) changes to them. Globals silently
# returned to their initial values; talloc_magic did, so talloc aborted with
# "Bad talloc magic value" in forked children and elsewhere, depending only on
# code layout. A first-priority constructor in talloc, linked into every Samba
# binary, turns fault-ahead off for the writable segment with MADV_RANDOM, and
# talloc_lib_init repeats it where NetBSD 4's GCC has no constructor priorities.
0046-talloc-data-faultahead.patch|keep static-data writes: no UVM fault-ahead on the writable segment
# Time Capsule HFS can report file and directory owner/group as 4294967295.
# Normalize only that sentinel at Samba's stat_ex boundary so ACL, stream, and
# security descriptor paths see the forced root/wheel appliance identity. The
# core-file directory setup calls lstat() directly, bypassing that
# normalization, so on appliance builds it uses the non-strict create, which
# skips both the owner and the 0700 checks; panics then produce disk-backed
# cores under logs/cores/smbd.
0023-stat-hfs-unknown-owner.patch|HFS unknown owner in corepath setup and stat metadata
# Time Capsule lock databases live on a tiny RAM disk. Add an smb.conf
# max-dead setting for every local dbwrap TDB (dbwrap_tdb_max_dead:*). Our 0
# sends volatile lock-database deletes straight to the freelist instead of
# retaining thousands of per-hash-chain dead records after Time Machine closes
# files; persistent TDBs already use 0, so only a positive value changes them.
0017-dbwrap-tdb-max-dead.patch|configurable dbwrap TDB max-dead records

# smbd without helper processes.
# Samba runs on the device as the smbd binary alone, about 10 MB of a 15 MB
# RAM disk, on a device with little memory. Stock smbd forks resident helpers
# (notifyd, cleanupd, the durable-handle scavenger) and a datagram-socket sweep
# every 15 minutes, and serves RPC pipes from the separate samba-dcerpcd and
# rpcd_* binaries. Here smbd is the parent, one child per client connection
# and, when VFS_AIO_FORK_ENABLED is on, aio_fork's helpers.
#
# Finder and smbclient -L list shares through IPC$ \PIPE\srvsvc; serve that
# one pipe inside smbd (overlay tc_embedded_srvsvc.c). Other pipes (lsarpc,
# samr, wkssvc, winreg) fall through to local_np_connect, which fails to start
# the absent samba-dcerpcd.
0005-rpc-embedded-srvsvc.patch|embedded srvsvc named pipe support
# On appliance builds (TC_SAMBA4X_APPLIANCE), run notifyd and cleanupd in the
# parent, as interactive smbd does, and the scavenger's durable-handle expiry
# timers there too (upstream forks it even in interactive mode). Skip the
# datagram-socket sweep, which forks each time it runs; cleanupd still removes
# each exited child's socket. Forked helpers work without pthread (tested
# 2026-09-26, tests/samba/VALIDATION.md); this saves two or three resident
# smbds (about 2.5 MB RSS each, some of it shared with the parent) and 14 KB
# of smbd.
0008-smbd-helpers-in-parent.patch|host smbd helper services in the parent event loop

# aio_fork.
# Normal helper retirement sends a short message without SCM_RIGHTS data.
# Keep that path valid under C's nonzero variable-length-array requirement.
# This is an upstream bug; it applies to pristine Samba.
0033-aio-fork-empty-control-message.patch|avoid zero-length arrays during aio_fork shutdown
# A helper's failed pread returned -1, which the parent cast to an oversized
# read and turned into EIO; return the helper's errno instead. Upstream bug.
0052-aio-fork-read-errno.patch|keep the helper's errno for a failed read
# Refactor only: pread, pwrite and fsync had three copies of the same
# send/done/recv code; use one path that takes the command.
0053-aio-fork-single-request-path.patch|one request path for pread, pwrite and fsync
# aio_fork:max_children bounds each tree connection's helpers (0 keeps
# upstream's no limit). Requests wait in a short FIFO for a free helper and are
# dispatched oldest first; past the FIFO bound, or when fork() fails, a request
# runs in the client's own smbd, which slows that client instead of failing its
# I/O. Each helper's shared buffer is 8 MiB, Samba's default SMB2 read and write
# size: stock aio_fork refused anything over 128 KiB with EINVAL.
#
# Only aio_fork_send (for a request queued alone) and the event loop (a tevent
# immediate, as tevent_queue does) run the FIFO: freeing, completing or failing
# a request never dispatches or completes another. A request freed while its
# helper works (smbd's teardown via assert_no_pending_aio, or a caller freeing
# it) leaves the helper busy until it replies; the reply is dropped and the
# helper reused (retired only if that read fails), as pthreadpool_tevent
# orphans a job, but by handing the helper the pending read instead of refusing
# the free. So a teardown forks, runs and completes nothing, and frees each
# helper once with its tree connection; stock aio_fork panicked (SMB_ASSERT)
# when a busy helper was freed. A helper stuck in a hung read keeps its slot
# until the read returns, as a pool thread would.
0031-aio-fork-max-children.patch|bounded aio_fork helpers with FIFO backpressure

# Time Machine.
# Ignore st_blocks drift on every durable reconnect: Time Machine's sparsebundle
# bands on HFS change their allocation-block count while the handle is
# disconnected. The other identity checks stay strict.
0019-durable-allow-block-count-drift.patch|allow durable reconnect across HFS allocation-block drift
# Time Machine can retry SMB2 durable opens before the old smbd has marked the
# open disconnected. Check that live record like a disconnected one and, for
# the same durable open only, retry outside the open-global TDB lock for up to
# about 5 seconds (34 x 150 ms) instead of reporting the handle as missing.
# FILE_NOT_AVAILABLE is only the internal "still busy" signal: once the retries
# run out the client gets OBJECT_NAME_NOT_FOUND, as upstream and MS-SMB2
# 3.3.5.9.12 give for an open still attached to a session (macOS revokes the
# file on any reconnect error). The wait sleeps in the reconnecting client's own
# smbd, which meanwhile answers nothing; other clients are not affected.
0024-durable-open-live-retry.patch|retry transient live durable-open reconnect races
# Not specific to Time Machine; an upstream bug that 0029 relies on. A shutdown
# close (a dropped connection, a bare SMB2 LOGOFF, a tree disconnect) runs from
# whatever working directory the last request left, which is "/" after any tree
# disconnect (close_cnum) or another share's root. Delete on close then finds
# no parent directory and leaves the file (upstream's wide-links check stops it
# resolving anywhere else), and 0029's by-name stat refuses the durable cookie.
# Change into the file's own share root first. A disk 0041 finds revoked stays
# safe even when Apple remounted it at the same path: both steps fstat the old
# descriptor before any lookup by name, and that fails on a revoked vnode.
0062-smbd-shutdown-close-share-root.patch|shutdown closes resolve names from the file's share root
# Time Capsule HFS can update ctime when the last writable fd closes. Record
# durable cookies after that close so reconnect keeps strict ctime validation
# without comparing against Samba's own stale pre-close snapshot (seen again
# 2026-09-27 during durable_device's macOS stall case). The post-close stat is
# by name, so it relies on 0062's share-root working directory.
0029-durable-post-close-cookie-stat.patch|record durable cookie stat after fd_close
# macOS Time Machine sends FSCTL_SRV_REQUEST_RESUME_KEY inside a compounded
# request while probing backup-disk capabilities. The default VFS finishes that
# read at once but delivers its callback from an immediate event, so fruit's
# offload_read wrapper and smb2_ioctl_network_fs each keep their parent request
# in progress, and the SMB2 compound guard rejects it with
# NT_STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR. Run the callback right away when the subrequest has
# already finished.
0028-ioctl-resume-key-sync-completion.patch|propagate synchronous resume-key IOCTL completion

# xattr_tdb and streams_xattr fixes.
# A file with no xattr_tdb row has an empty attribute set: report ENOATTR for
# it, not EINVAL, to every caller of the library. Stock vfs_xattr_tdb's fgetxattr
# already maps its errors to ENOATTR; EINVAL escaped through getxattrat (async
# dosmode) and to the library's other callers, such as the metadata migrator.
# Since 0038, HFS shares do not use this library. It also frees the stack frame
# that xattr_tdb_setattr leaked when it could not lock the row.
0018-xattr-tdb-missing-row-empty.patch|xattr_tdb missing rows behave like empty xattrs
# macOS/iOS Photos writes many assetsd metadata streams. Once xattr_tdb's
# stream-name list exceeds Samba's initial 1024-byte listxattr buffer, return
# the normal -1/ERANGE retry signal instead of a positive required length that
# callers mistake for bytes written into the too-small buffer.
0027-xattr-tdb-listxattr-erange.patch|xattr_tdb retries oversized xattr name lists
# With xattr_tdb:ignore_user_xattr, user xattr reads pass to the next module
# before xattr_tdb has built its local smb_fname copy, so upstream passes NULL.
# Pass the caller's name instead.
0047-xattr-tdb-getxattrat-bypass-name.patch|pass the caller's name when xattr_tdb bypasses a user xattr read
# Native HFS xattrs hold at most 3802 bytes, so smb.conf sets 35 xattrs per
# stream to keep the previous ~128 KiB Windows alternate data stream limit.
# Upstream accepts at most 16.
0048-streams-xattr-max-64-per-stream.patch|allow up to 64 xattrs per stream

# Native Mac metadata.
# Apple firmware stores AFP FinderInfo and ordinary Mac xattrs in native HFS
# attributes that its SMB server reaches only through private xattr syscalls
# (overlay airport_native_xattr.h), and resource forks in the file's resource
# fork, reached as <file>/..namedfork/rsrc. Store them there so AFP and SMB see
# one copy: fruit owns FinderInfo and resource-fork stream semantics, xattr_tdb
# owns ordinary native attributes.
#
# On an HFS share, xattr_tdb keeps ordinary xattrs and Apple's com.apple.*
# streams in native attributes instead of its database, and leaves FinderInfo
# and resource forks to fruit.
0038-xattr-tdb-native-hfs.patch|native HFS attributes for xattr_tdb
# Apple's com.apple.* streams are one native attribute without the trailing
# NUL that streams_xattr stores, so allow that byte and refuse a larger value
# (E2BIG) instead of splitting it across extents: AFP must see the whole
# attribute. Windows streams keep their extent layout.
0054-streams-xattr-native-apple-streams.patch|whole native attributes for Apple streams
# On an HFS share fruit ignores fruit:metadata and serves the AFP_AfpInfo
# stream from the native com.apple.FinderInfo attribute (overlay
# tc_fruit_native_finderinfo.c); an all-zero value counts as absent, as it does
# for AFP. It also skips AppleDouble conversion there: fruit never reads ._
# files on HFS. The deploy migrator converts them, but only on a volume that
# still has a legacy xattr.tdb (every version that wrote ._ files, from commit
# b18c5901 until native HFS in 2629eaec, also wrote one), which saves a
# whole-disk walk on every deploy.
# Uninstall removes the payload with that database, so ._ files on a disk
# uninstalled and reinstalled stay unconverted and hidden. The patch also
# zero-fills fruit_pwrite_meta's AfpInfo buffer, so a 17-59 byte write no
# longer stores uninitialized stack bytes (an upstream bug).
0055-fruit-native-finderinfo.patch|native HFS FinderInfo in fruit
# Building on 0055's native_hfs switch, fruit serves the AFP_Resource stream
# from the file's native resource fork, opened as <file>/..namedfork/rsrc like
# AFP does (overlay tc_fruit_native_rsrc.c). On an HFS share fruit:resource is
# ignored.
0056-fruit-native-resource-fork.patch|native HFS resource forks in fruit
# The static helper in overlay tc_xattr_hfs_migrate.c moves legacy
# TDB/AppleDouble metadata during deploy only, on every deploy while legacy
# databases remain; it merges several historical databases without changing
# source mtimes and keeps anything it cannot prove orphaned. Values larger than
# a native attribute stay in legacy storage as completed metadata. It sets
# aside (never deletes) databases whose remaining rows are proven orphans or
# such values, keeps those values' ._ files, and deletes only fully migrated
# databases.
0057-xattr-hfs-migrate-build.patch|build the deploy-only metadata migrator

# Apple disk rebinding.
# Apple can revoke a bumped USB disk's vnode and remount the same dkN. Record
# each tree's volume identity at connect, forward the manager's reload signal
# from the parent to every worker, and disconnect only trees whose disk binding
# was revoked, replaced or removed; other shares on the session stay connected
# (overlay source3/smbd/tc_disk_bindings.c).
0041-smbd-stale-tree-reload.patch|forward parent reloads and disconnect only revoked Apple disk bindings

# Native symlinks.
# Apple's AFP server and SSH store real POSIX symlinks on these disks, while
# macOS SMB clients create 1067-byte Minshall+French "XSym" files. Keep the
# disk native (tc:native symlinks, set by the unified service's smb.conf).
# Existing XSym files stay as they are; there is no migration.
#
# List native links to every SMB client as symlink reparse points, and treat a
# terminal link as the object for macOS (it resolves links itself and renames
# without FILE_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT). A link is opened as itself without a
# descriptor and stat'ed with lstat; its target is mapped into the client's
# namespace (an absolute target is kept verbatim); AAPL clients get the reparse
# tag, not max_access, in EaSize; and non-Mac clients see a link to a directory
# with FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY.
0045-smbd-native-links-reparse-points.patch|native POSIX symlinks as reparse points
# Map catia's private-use characters in a link's own name when links are read
# and created.
0058-catia-native-link-names.patch|catia name mapping for link reads and creates
# Keep a link's streams, xattrs and times on the link itself, never its
# target: the native HFS backend reaches a link handle, which has no
# descriptor, by path, and stream deletion skips links. vfs_default's path
# fallbacks never follow a link, on every share: chown and times act on the
# link itself, while mode, flags and xattr writes fail with ENOTSUP, an xattr
# read with ENOATTR, and a listing is empty.
0059-smbd-native-links-own-metadata.patch|a link keeps its own streams, xattrs and times
# When the creating client explicitly closes the only handle to a file it
# created (a shutdown close never converts), replace an XSym file, or a
# placeholder carrying a symlink reparse payload (Windows, Linux
# symlink=native/nfs/wsl), with a native link under the share mode lock, never
# replacing another object, and carry its metadata over except Finder info; a
# file with a resource fork stays a file. The original is removed only after its
# fd closes, and each conversion ends with sync(): without it the devices' HFS
# journal panicked within a few test cycles ("jnl: start_tr: active_tr is NULL",
# a kernel bug that then fires in whichever process ends the next transaction).
# A Mac (AAPL) reading a native link's data gets the equivalent XSym bytes,
# whoever made the link, and writes that leave them unchanged succeed, so a Mac
# still caching the XSym file it wrote keeps working. Other reparse tags (FIFOs,
# sockets, devices, junctions) are refused; mklink /D stays refused by
# upstream's regular-file check. Only non-Mac connections see
# FILE_SUPPORTS_REPARSE_POINTS, so macOS keeps creating XSym.
0060-smbd-native-links-convert-on-close.patch|new SMB links become native links when their creator closes them

# Diagnostics.
# Log each step of a client's durable reconnect at level 3, prefixed
# tc_reconnect: (SMBD_DEBUG_LOGGING shows it): negotiate with its client GUID,
# the session setup and the previous session it names, why close_previous acted
# or left that session alone, the old smbd closing it, each durable open it
# disconnects, the reconnect request and its result, and why connections end.
0061-smbd-reconnect-diagnostics.patch|durable reconnect diagnostics at log level 3
